      "independent_solution": "For each part, construct the line and parabola on common axes and obtain roots as all intersection abscissae, not their y-coordinates. Equivalently divide all terms by nonzero a and reuse y=x^2: a) line y=4-3x gives roots -4 and 1; b) line y=2x+5 gives approximate graph roots -1.45 and 3.45, with exact checks 1-sqrt(6) and 1+sqrt(6); c) line y=-2x-5 stays strictly below y=x^2 on the entire real axis, since the gap is (x+1)^2+4, so there are no real intersections or real roots; d) line y=x/2+3/2 gives -1 and 3/2; e) line y=-2x/3+5/3 gives -5/3 and 1; f) line y=-12x/5+2 gives graph roots about -3.05 and 0.65, with exact checks (-6-sqrt(86))/5 and (-6+sqrt(86))/5. Preserve both branches and both roots even when one is far left or fractional. The unnormalized pairs y=2x^2, y=x+3 in d; y=3x^2, y=-2x+5 in e; and y=5x^2, y=-12x+10 in f have the same root abscissae but scaled ordinates. Algebra below only verifies graphical solutions; formula-only replacement would omit the explicit source method.",
