    {
      "source_number": "854",
      "independent_solution": "For real x,y the original denominators require x+y!=0 and y-x!=0, since y^2-x^2=(y-x)(x+y). On this domain, the parenthesized divisor has common numerator 2xy+x(y-x)-y(x+y)=-(y-x)^2 and equals -(y-x)/(x+y), which is nonzero. Therefore the whole expression equals [(y-x)^2/(x+y)]/[ -(y-x)/(x+y) ]=-(y-x)=x-y. At x=8.4,y=-0.6, x+y=7.8, y-x=-9, y^2-x^2=-70.2, so the input is valid and the result is 9. Independently, the divisor is 28/195+14/13-1/15=15/13 and the outer numerator fraction is 135/13; their quotient is 9.",
      "coverage": {
        "verdict": "pass",
        "evidence": "The complete original expression and both given decimal inputs are displayed, with one whole-value response. Author comparison: Author coverage retains the whole expression, both signed inputs and connected division task, while declaring lack of written algebra and denominator-check production. No term or original input is removed."
      }
